1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,120
Solitude is not a private, therapeutic place.

2
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:23,180
Rather, it is the place of conversion.

3
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:28,880
The place where the old self dies.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,280
And the new self is born.

5
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:40,260
Solitude is the furnace of transformation.

6
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:49,380
Without solitude, we remain victims of our society.

7
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:54,520
And continue to be entangled in the illusion of the false self.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,300
Solitude is the place of the great struggle and the great encounter.

9
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:20,160
Church, good to not hear from you this morning.

10
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:23,360
So glad you've joined us.

11
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:24,600
Hey, I don't do this very often.

12
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:26,380
Actually, I don't know that I've ever done it.

13
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:30,160
But the Scripture says to outdo one another in showing honor.

14
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:34,040
And I heard a story this morning that I think is worthy of honor.

15
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:35,580
They have no clue that I'm sharing this.

16
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:44,940
But my friend Mike up here on the front row, regular, almost every week, wheels his motorized scooter here to church.

17
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:48,060
And this morning, his motorized scooter stopped working.

18
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:55,080
And you wheeled yourself probably over a mile in a manual wheelchair to get here and be in the presence of God this morning.

19
00:01:55,180 --> 00:01:56,460
So I just want to honor you for that.

20
00:01:56,460 --> 00:02:03,040
I thought getting my toddler into the car was a difficulty.

21
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:06,260
Hey, if we haven't met yet, my name is Ryan.

22
00:02:06,260 --> 00:02:08,180
I have the privilege of being one of the pastors here.

23
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:12,800
And this week, we are launching, as you saw from that hipster video we just played,

24
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:19,900
that we are launching a new four-week series on the spiritual practice of silence and solitude.

25
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:25,640
If you are new or newer to Canvas, this is a part of a larger series that we are doing over three years.

26
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:30,900
We started it last year on the spiritual practices of the life and teaching of Jesus.

27
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:35,720
We didn't think that hitting you with all of these at one time would be wise.

28
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:36,860
And so we've spread them out.

29
00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:40,020
Last year, we started with prayer and fasting and with generosity.

30
00:02:40,020 --> 00:02:46,460
And so if you're interested in those practices and weren't here, by God's grace and the skill of our tech team,

31
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:47,920
they are available online.

32
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:51,040
So if you want to go back and find those, we'd love to encourage you to do that.

33
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:58,520
And at the beginning of this series, I did an entire message on the importance of practice-based discipleship.

34
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:01,500
I don't have time to recap that entire sermon.

35
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:04,840
I actually was planning on doing that last week, but we got a snowstorm.

36
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:11,920
And so now I'm going to ask you that if you have questions about or are wondering about what practice-based discipleship is all about,

37
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:13,720
that you kind of go back and find that.

38
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:15,280
We'll put it in the newsletter this week.

39
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:21,320
But just to make sure that we're all on the same page, I will do a small amount of recap.

40
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:30,500
And I'll start by saying that I personally feel like much of American Christianity, what we call discipleship, is rooted in our intellect.

41
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:38,260
We have become consumed with learning about God, which is a great pursuit and a pursuit that the Scriptures encourage.

42
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:41,480
But it's only one facet of true discipleship.

43
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:49,680
Scripture makes it clear that we are to be doers of the Word and not just hearers only, not just to intellectualize ideas about God,

44
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:53,640
but to allow them to transform us from the very inside of our being.

45
00:03:53,640 --> 00:04:00,340
And discipleship in the eyes of Jesus and in the authors of Scripture, it was a whole person endeavor.

46
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:03,960
I heard a quote this week from a guy named John Ortberg, and he said,

47
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:07,060
God is not interested in your spiritual life.

48
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:09,900
He is interested in your life.

49
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:16,960
And we have this way in Western American culture of separating our spirituality from the rest of our lives.

50
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:23,100
And if we believe the Scriptures, the Scriptures are clear that all of our entire being is a spiritual being,

51
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:26,120
that we were created in the image of God, created for relationship with God.

52
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:30,760
There is no separation between spiritual things and normal things.

53
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:32,080
It's all God's.

54
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:41,920
And so when discipleship just becomes about the mind, we really segment our discipleship into a way that it never was meant to be.

55
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:44,640
It's a whole person endeavor.

56
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:50,880
And for Jesus, discipleship was an institution that existed where people would follow around a rabbi,

57
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:53,200
spending time with them, learning their teachings.

58
00:04:53,200 --> 00:05:00,480
Yes, learning their teachings intellectually, but growing in understanding and also modeling their lifestyle.

59
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:01,880
Emulating their lifestyle.

60
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:06,440
So in order for us to take this approach and contextualize it to American Christianity,

61
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:12,380
we summarized in that message I just talked about discipleship by saying it's all about being with Jesus,

62
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:17,680
becoming like Jesus so that we can do the things that Jesus did.

63
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:22,080
This really is a summary of what faithful Christianity should look like.

64
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:29,220
But unfortunately, oftentimes, we've segmented faithful Christianity into the information that we learn and internalize.

65
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:33,800
And the verses that we can quote back to people and the systematic theology that we believe,

66
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:37,720
whether or not it actually transforms us into a person of Christ-like love.

67
00:05:37,720 --> 00:05:48,180
So in order for us to do this, we have to expand the discipleship process from beyond just solely being focused on the mind to being focused on the whole self.

68
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:49,260
This is not a new idea.

69
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:53,380
You guys probably will find this verse from Deuteronomy familiar.

70
00:05:53,380 --> 00:05:53,820
It says,

71
00:05:53,820 --> 00:06:00,660
Love the Lord your God with all of your heart, soul, mind, and strength.

72
00:06:00,660 --> 00:06:02,800
It's a whole person endeavor.

73
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:05,560
Later, the Apostle Paul will pray this prayer.

74
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:07,720
He prays this to the church at Ephesus.

75
00:06:07,860 --> 00:06:09,240
He says,

76
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:15,100
I pray that you would know the love of Christ that passes understanding.

77
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:22,940
So there's this form of discipleship, this form of a relationship with Jesus that goes beyond just what we can mentally comprehend.

78
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:24,640
We are whole people.

79
00:06:26,540 --> 00:06:29,160
These practices, we've picked nine.

80
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:33,900
It's part of a larger church-wide curriculum that will go with our small group.

81
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:37,400
So if you join a small group, you'll continue to learn more about these practices.

82
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:45,220
But they serve as a holistic training routine aimed at helping us to do this very thing, love God with our whole selves.

83
00:06:45,220 --> 00:06:46,780
They help us do what Paul commanded.

84
00:06:46,780 --> 00:06:49,640
Train yourself in godliness.

85
00:06:49,640 --> 00:06:56,480
It's a whole person endeavor so that we can be conformed into the image of Jesus.

86
00:06:56,480 --> 00:06:58,100
This does not happen on accident.

87
00:06:58,100 --> 00:07:05,960
It is a long and slow process of regular intentionality, of bringing our whole selves before God to be transformed through the work of his spirit.

88
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:08,700
Now, we don't do the work.

89
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:10,280
It's important that we distinguish this.

90
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:11,680
We don't do the work.

91
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:15,140
We position ourselves before God for him to do the work in us.

92
00:07:15,140 --> 00:07:19,720
Like I said, up to this point, we've talked about prayer, fasting, and generosity.

93
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:26,300
Each of these practices help us position different parts of ourselves before God to be transformed into the image of Jesus.

94
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:35,200
And this month, we will look at solitude, which is slowly rising up the power rankings of my spiritual practices.

95
00:07:35,540 --> 00:07:38,860
And that should say a lot to you because I am a millennial extrovert.

96
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:43,520
That joke landed better when I wrote it down.

97
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:47,180
All right, let's pray and get started.

98
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:55,540
Father in heaven, thank you for the humility that this group of people give me by not laughing at my jokes.

99
00:07:55,540 --> 00:07:59,860
Thank you for your word, for your love.

100
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:09,540
That is for our whole selves, for our whole lives, not just for one segment of our life or one hour in the morning and one hour in the evening or one hour on a Sunday morning.

101
00:08:09,540 --> 00:08:11,360
That you care about all of it.

102
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:27,280
God, may this study of and practice of solitude just be one asset in that endeavor.

103
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:36,960
One helpful tool that allows us to position ourselves before you, to hear from you, and to grow into the Christ-like love that you are calling us to.

104
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:46,500
God, may you transform us as people into your image so that we can be what Jesus wants us to be, a city on a hill, salt on the earth.

105
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:48,480
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

106
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:49,280
Amen.

107
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:56,980
If you want to turn in your Bibles to Luke chapter 5, we'll be in Luke chapter 5, and then later we'll be in Mark chapter 6.

108
00:08:56,980 --> 00:09:02,560
As you're turning there, a few months ago I was listening to the testimony of a girl by the name of Brenna.

109
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:10,340
And Brenna had grown up in the church but had some really traumatic experiences and decided to walk away from God altogether.

110
00:09:10,340 --> 00:09:13,280
She's what many of us would consider a prodigal.

111
00:09:14,980 --> 00:09:23,080
And she had this period, this season of being a prodigal, of running away from God, of choosing to do the things that she wanted to do the way that she wanted to do them.

112
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:30,160
And then she had a radical, actually multiple radical encounters with the grace of God that transformed her mind and her heart.

113
00:09:30,160 --> 00:09:32,240
And she repented and she turned back to God.

114
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:35,140
And she ended up becoming a youth pastor.

115
00:09:35,140 --> 00:09:44,280
Now, in the midst of her conversion, in the midst of her radical encounter with God's grace, she still, because of her trauma and because of her past experiences,

116
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:49,200
continued to wrestle with deep mental health issues.

117
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:55,960
She was diagnosed with bipolar and she wrestled with bouts of depression, dark, deep depression.

118
00:09:55,960 --> 00:10:03,200
And there came to a point while she was pastoring at a church, she was leading an entire youth group of church kids,

119
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:07,240
that she came to the realization that she could not go on any longer.

120
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:13,360
And she decided that that day, the day that she made the decision, that she was going to take her own life.

121
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:17,800
She was going to go to the church office so that her husband didn't think anything was off.

122
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:20,620
She was going to tell her pastor that she was considering resigning.

123
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:28,880
She was going to go home so that she had more than enough time to complete the action that she had planned before her husband got home.

124
00:10:29,160 --> 00:10:34,680
She went to the pastor's office, told him, thinking about resigning, I need to go home.

125
00:10:34,680 --> 00:10:35,960
He canceled all her meetings.

126
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:42,360
And as she was packing up her stuff in the office, she put her backpack on and she turned the corner.

127
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:48,160
And there standing in the doorframe of her office was a woman that she did not know very well.

128
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:53,820
And that woman said, Brenna, do you need me to take you to the hospital?

129
00:10:53,820 --> 00:10:58,060
Brenna said, no, I'm fine.

130
00:10:58,060 --> 00:10:58,860
Thank you.

131
00:10:58,860 --> 00:10:59,700
I appreciate it.

132
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:00,340
I'm good.

133
00:11:00,340 --> 00:11:08,040
And the woman continued and said, is there any belief in the idea that someone might be able to help you?

134
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:16,760
Now, the crazy part about this story is this woman wasn't particularly close to Brenna and had no real knowledge of what was going on in Brenna's life.

135
00:11:16,760 --> 00:11:27,920
She was just a woman who had been faithful in prayer and was driving past the church, praying for Brenna, prompted by the Holy Spirit, and felt like she needed to go and check on Brenna.

136
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:32,400
That story saved her life.

137
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:38,240
And I don't know about you, but when I hear stories like this, I am confronted with two very different emotions.

138
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:43,020
The first emotion is the godly, sanctified emotion.

139
00:11:43,020 --> 00:11:47,380
And I am overcome with the love of God for us as individuals.

140
00:11:47,380 --> 00:11:49,300
He knows us.

141
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:50,100
He sees us.

142
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:50,940
He loves us.

143
00:11:50,940 --> 00:11:54,380
He cares about us each as individual children of God.

144
00:11:54,380 --> 00:11:55,520
I am overwhelmed.

145
00:11:55,520 --> 00:11:57,880
I was sobbing in my car as I heard this story.

146
00:11:57,880 --> 00:12:07,700
And yet, quickly, because I am a real person with a real life and I struggle with all kinds of sin, the second emotion that I wrestle with is envy.

147
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:11,240
Where are these stories in my life?

148
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:14,680
Why don't I hear from God like this?

149
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:18,080
Why doesn't God use me in this way?

150
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:21,140
Real people, real life.

151
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:28,340
But the more I reflect on that second emotion, the more I think about that question and the posture of my daily life,

152
00:12:28,380 --> 00:12:35,940
the more I am led to consider that the most likely scenario isn't that God isn't speaking to me or inviting me into stories like this,

153
00:12:35,940 --> 00:12:39,800
but rather that I'm just not very intentional about listening for his invitation.

154
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:53,460
Which leads me to the question, is there, might there be a practice from the life and teachings of Jesus that could position us to hear from God and to be used by him in ways that we've only ever dreamt of?

155
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:56,600
Anybody have any guesses?

156
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:59,720
Solitude.

157
00:12:59,720 --> 00:13:00,720
Yes.

158
00:13:00,720 --> 00:13:06,520
Over the next four weeks, we will be looking at and inviting you to participate in a practice that is central.

159
00:13:06,520 --> 00:13:13,780
If you read the Gospels, this practice of getting away alone with God in prayer is central to the life and teachings of Jesus.

160
00:13:13,940 --> 00:13:20,900
And not only that, up until this point in Western Christianity, it has historically been central to the life and teachings of the church.

161
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:24,040
Read the church fathers, the church mothers, read church history.

162
00:13:24,040 --> 00:13:29,220
Solitude was a regular practice of Christians throughout the century.

163
00:13:29,220 --> 00:13:31,720
I love how John Mark Comer puts it.

164
00:13:31,720 --> 00:13:39,780
He says, all over the life of Jesus and pretty much all of the saints and sages from every century, every era, and every stream of the church,

165
00:13:39,780 --> 00:13:43,800
here comes the condemnation, except for American evangelicalism,

166
00:13:43,920 --> 00:13:48,500
sings in chorus that solitude is foundational to the spiritual life.

167
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:56,620
Getting alone with God, emptying ourselves of our agendas and our thoughts and our things,

168
00:13:56,620 --> 00:14:01,160
and letting God speak directly to us has been foundational to the spiritual life.

169
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:07,060
So, let's hop into Luke chapter 5 and see this example from Christ's life.

170
00:14:07,060 --> 00:14:09,540
Luke chapter 5, starting in verse 15.

171
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:19,920
So that crowds of people came to hear him and to be healed of their sicknesses.

172
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:21,800
Jesus' ministry is picking up steam.

173
00:14:21,800 --> 00:14:22,680
People are being healed.

174
00:14:22,680 --> 00:14:30,040
Crowds are coming from miles and miles away to meet this Jesus and to see this power that he has over sickness.

175
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:37,560
But Luke very intentionally puts this phrase in here for us to help us see a pattern that was true of Jesus' life.

176
00:14:37,640 --> 00:14:40,340
Even as his popularity was growing, it says,

177
00:14:40,340 --> 00:14:46,320
But Jesus often withdrew to lonely places and prayed.

178
00:14:47,920 --> 00:15:06,900
As the success, as the popularity of Christ's ministry goes viral, Luke intentionally gives us this summary statement about the pattern of Christ's life and the busyness of his mission.

179
00:15:06,980 --> 00:15:12,240
Listen to this, Jesus' mission on earth was to save the world from sin and death.

180
00:15:12,240 --> 00:15:15,060
Sounds pretty important, right?

181
00:15:16,020 --> 00:15:26,200
Sounds like efficiency, sounds like using your time wisely is a really important thing that you would want to have nailed down if your job in life was to save the world from their sins.

182
00:15:26,200 --> 00:15:32,300
And yet he regularly and intentionally withdrew to lonely places to pray.

183
00:15:32,300 --> 00:15:34,440
This word in Greek, I taught the worship team.

184
00:15:34,440 --> 00:15:35,260
They were like, what does it mean?

185
00:15:35,300 --> 00:15:36,860
I'm like, well, just pay attention to the sermon.

186
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:37,420
You'll find out.

187
00:15:37,420 --> 00:15:43,900
The Greek word here that is translated in the NIV as lonely places is oramos.

188
00:15:43,900 --> 00:15:50,180
Literally, it can be translated as desert or the desolate place or the wilderness.

189
00:15:50,180 --> 00:15:55,760
The idea here is it is a place where flourishing life cannot be sustained.

190
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:57,700
No one lived in the desert.

191
00:15:57,700 --> 00:15:59,920
You only passed through the desert.

192
00:16:01,020 --> 00:16:06,560
It's a place, the only place, where you could be free from all other inputs or distractions.

193
00:16:06,560 --> 00:16:11,900
And as you read the Gospels, you will see that it seems to be one of Christ's favorite places.

194
00:16:11,900 --> 00:16:14,500
Jesus was not a hermit.

195
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:26,760
He wasn't always in the desert, but he had this pattern, this cycle of going into, leaning into community with people who are needy and broken and who needed God's grace in their life and then withdrawing to solitude in prayer.

196
00:16:26,760 --> 00:16:30,100
I love how Eugene Peterson paraphrases this verse.

197
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:37,460
He says, as often as possible, Jesus withdrew to out-of-the-way places for prayer.

198
00:16:37,460 --> 00:16:49,520
Jesus made it an intentional practice to get away from the noise and the busyness of the world and of his ministry and of his occupation, his job, and he got away to be alone with God.

199
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:52,960
Now, I know what you might be thinking.

200
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:55,200
He was Jesus.

201
00:16:55,200 --> 00:16:58,540
Good for him, right?

202
00:16:59,740 --> 00:17:07,000
But not only is it an example that Jesus gave us, but it was an invitation that he extended to his followers.

203
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,680
Mark chapter 6, verse 30.

204
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:15,260
The apostles gathered around Jesus and reported to him all that they had done and taught.

205
00:17:15,320 --> 00:17:21,240
So this is after Jesus sends out the apostles two by two to go and to begin to do what Jesus did, right?

206
00:17:21,240 --> 00:17:25,460
They're in this process of being with Jesus, becoming like Jesus, and doing what Jesus did.

207
00:17:25,460 --> 00:17:28,440
He sends them out to do what he has done.

208
00:17:28,440 --> 00:17:31,080
They have incredible success, right?

209
00:17:31,080 --> 00:17:32,380
They're seeing people healed.

210
00:17:32,380 --> 00:17:35,160
They're seeing people come to the knowledge of the kingdom of God.

211
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:38,660
And they're celebrating this with Jesus.

212
00:17:38,660 --> 00:17:43,420
This would be a time, if I was Jesus, where I would be stoked and celebrating all that God is doing.

213
00:17:43,420 --> 00:17:46,120
But Jesus doesn't seem to be that concerned with their success.

214
00:17:46,120 --> 00:17:47,440
Look what it says in verse 31.

215
00:17:47,620 --> 00:17:52,460
Because so many people were coming and going, and they did not even have a chance to eat.

216
00:17:52,460 --> 00:17:59,920
He said to them, come away with me by yourselves to the Eremoth, a quiet place, and get some rest.

217
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:05,200
So not only is the wilderness a place to get away and pray, but it's also a place to get away and rest.

218
00:18:06,040 --> 00:18:10,720
They went away by themselves in a boat to a solitary place, the Eremoth.

219
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:17,020
But many who saw them leaving recognized them and ran on foot to the towns to go ahead of them.

220
00:18:17,020 --> 00:18:29,620
So not only does Jesus model this pattern of solitude in his life for us, but he extends the invitation to his followers to come away, to a lonely place, to a desolate place.

221
00:18:29,620 --> 00:18:34,880
Solitude and silence is not an extra credit assignment for the spiritually elite.

222
00:18:35,860 --> 00:18:37,280
This is what I grew up thinking.

223
00:18:37,280 --> 00:18:43,900
I grew up thinking that good Christians, we read a verse of the day or a devotional in the morning.

224
00:18:43,900 --> 00:18:46,860
We pray before meals, and then we're good.

225
00:18:46,860 --> 00:18:47,340
Check.

226
00:18:47,340 --> 00:18:48,520
I did it.

227
00:18:48,520 --> 00:18:53,700
But there's so much more to the life of discipleship with Jesus that we're invited into.

228
00:18:53,700 --> 00:18:56,600
And we're going to talk about this a little bit more later, but it is an invitation.

229
00:18:56,600 --> 00:18:59,680
It is not a legalistic box for you to check.

230
00:18:59,680 --> 00:19:05,680
This is an invitation Jesus is extending to each and every one of you to make space, to get away, to draw back.

231
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:11,120
To draw back from the busyness of this life and to just clear your mind and your heart and let him speak.

232
00:19:12,660 --> 00:19:25,580
It's interesting to me that both of these passages come after a season of busyness and success and where crowds are coming in and where the world is drawing them into a season that they could be busier and busier and busier.

233
00:19:25,580 --> 00:19:36,420
But after this season of busyness and success, it's almost as if Jesus knows the thing they need more than anything is to get away and to recenter themselves in God's presence.

234
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:40,520
To be reminded of their identity as children of God.

235
00:19:40,520 --> 00:19:44,020
To be reminded of what actually matters in life.

236
00:19:44,520 --> 00:19:49,440
It's almost like Jesus created us and he knows what we need for a flourishing life.

237
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:57,240
Success, busyness, and distraction, they all have a way of making us feel far more important than we actually are.

238
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:00,800
Far more anxious than we actually should be.

239
00:20:00,800 --> 00:20:03,660
Far more overwhelmed by the difficulties of life.

240
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:09,800
And solitude keeps us from that lie of the enemy.

241
00:20:09,800 --> 00:20:12,460
It keeps us centered on the thing that matters most.

242
00:20:15,140 --> 00:20:23,980
We live in a culture whose currents, right, the currents of our culture are always pulling us towards busyness and efficiency.

243
00:20:23,980 --> 00:20:28,120
It's all about what you can accomplish and how quickly you can accomplish it.

244
00:20:28,120 --> 00:20:42,740
And when you aren't busy, there is an entire industry led by some of the smartest and most powerful people in the world hiding behind a six-inch screen in your pocket and a 60-inch screen on the most prominent wall in your house.

245
00:20:43,540 --> 00:20:53,780
And their entire pursuit, the smartest, most powerful, wealthy people in our culture, their entire goal in life is to distract you from real life all around you.

246
00:20:53,780 --> 00:21:08,920
We're constantly being ripped away into busyness, into distraction, to distract us from the life all around us.

247
00:21:11,420 --> 00:21:19,180
Not only is this the norm of the culture, but if you consider to swim upstream of this, you would likely be considered a weirdo.

248
00:21:19,180 --> 00:21:24,340
It's no wonder we're exhausted, we're anxious, we're overwhelmed, many of us.

249
00:21:24,340 --> 00:21:25,840
We would long to hear from God.

250
00:21:25,840 --> 00:21:32,740
We would love to hear from God, but we don't even have the energy to have a meaningful conversation with our spouse and loved ones at the end of a long day.

251
00:21:34,040 --> 00:21:43,340
How are we going to hear from God who often speaks with a whisper when we're so exhausted and burnt out and distracted by the busyness of life?

252
00:21:43,340 --> 00:21:45,740
And I wrote that line for myself.

253
00:21:45,740 --> 00:21:52,920
Often I get home and I can't even engage my wife with a meaningful conversation because of how emotionally drained and exhausted I am.

254
00:21:52,920 --> 00:21:55,940
How am I ever going to hear from a God who often speaks with a whisper?

255
00:22:00,220 --> 00:22:05,860
The question for us then becomes, are we content to be conformed to the patterns of this world?

256
00:22:05,860 --> 00:22:13,680
Or are we willing to make upstream decisions that position us to be transformed into a person of Christ-like love by the power of the Holy Spirit?

257
00:22:13,680 --> 00:22:22,040
To put it another way, to steal some language from Jesus, do we want to follow the crowd down the wide road that leads to destruction?

258
00:22:22,680 --> 00:22:25,840
Or will we choose the road less traveled that leads to life?

259
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:38,280
If you're like me and you prefer to take the road less traveled, I think we should seriously consider the example and invitation of Jesus to get away to lonely places and pray.

260
00:22:38,280 --> 00:22:41,040
This won't be easy, nor will it be a quick fix.

261
00:22:41,040 --> 00:22:49,500
It will require us to declutter our schedules or at the least reclaim a healthy relationship with our devices.

262
00:22:52,580 --> 00:23:01,920
And I know as a young person up here talking about this, you might think that I'm talking to the younger generation, but I've hung out with some of you boomers.

263
00:23:01,920 --> 00:23:07,920
We are not the only ones that have an unhealthy relationship with our devices.

264
00:23:07,920 --> 00:23:13,580
We have an unhealthy relationship with our devices, but we aren't the only ones, okay?

265
00:23:13,580 --> 00:23:17,200
Equal opportunity offender.

266
00:23:17,200 --> 00:23:21,220
Again, it's not easy.

267
00:23:21,220 --> 00:23:22,180
It's not a quick fix.

268
00:23:22,480 --> 00:23:24,080
It isn't one size fits all.

269
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:31,860
You'll have to find a way to create the Eremos in your life based on your circumstances and your season of life and your personality.

270
00:23:31,860 --> 00:23:41,960
But we have to be willing, if we want to step off of the wide road that leads to destruction, the road that our culture's currents are pushing us towards.

271
00:23:41,960 --> 00:23:48,600
If we want to step off that road and into the narrow road that leads to life, we'll have to make intentional, difficult decisions.

272
00:23:49,400 --> 00:23:50,960
Again, I quoted John Ortberg earlier.

273
00:23:50,960 --> 00:23:57,160
When he was a young, successful pastor of a growing church, he wanted to make sure that he was keeping watch of his soul.

274
00:23:57,160 --> 00:23:59,940
And so he went to an old mentor by the name of Dallas Willard.

275
00:24:00,140 --> 00:24:01,620
Some of you are familiar with his work.

276
00:24:01,620 --> 00:24:05,680
He is foundational in this idea of practice-based discipleship.

277
00:24:05,680 --> 00:24:12,360
And Ortberg went to Willard and said, what do I need to do to have a spiritually healthy life?

278
00:24:12,360 --> 00:24:18,300
Thinking he was going to get some profound knowledge or wisdom, Willard responded with this one line.

279
00:24:18,960 --> 00:24:22,380
You must ruthlessly eliminate hurry from your life.

280
00:24:22,380 --> 00:24:29,620
And this is the example and the invitation we see of Jesus when busyness and when crowds were coming all around.

281
00:24:29,620 --> 00:24:36,260
It was Jesus' natural inclination to withdraw from the busyness, to get away, to recenter himself in the presence of God.

282
00:24:36,260 --> 00:24:41,660
It's his invitation to us when we are overwhelmed by the busyness of life, the distractions of life.

283
00:24:43,440 --> 00:24:47,520
To get away, to a lonely place, to rest a while and pray.

284
00:24:47,520 --> 00:24:50,820
Mother Teresa says it this way.

285
00:24:50,820 --> 00:24:56,100
She says, we need to find God and he cannot be found in noise and restlessness.

286
00:24:56,100 --> 00:24:58,920
God is the friend of silence.

287
00:24:58,920 --> 00:25:03,140
A God who often speaks in whispers.

288
00:25:03,140 --> 00:25:09,600
How will we ever hear him if we never quiet our minds and our hearts and allow him to speak in the way that he tends to speak?

289
00:25:09,600 --> 00:25:13,420
I'm not saying he only ever speaks in a whisper that he won't break through the messiness of your life.

290
00:25:13,440 --> 00:25:14,960
The distractions of your life.

291
00:25:14,960 --> 00:25:22,560
But if we want to be a people who regularly hear from God and respond to his invitation, I think we have to take this invitation of solitude seriously.

292
00:25:22,560 --> 00:25:32,600
Solitude is one of the only things in the spiritual life that remind us that we are totally loved by God.

293
00:25:32,600 --> 00:25:34,420
Not because of anything that we do.

294
00:25:35,460 --> 00:25:38,080
Our presence, our presence, our showing up is enough.

295
00:25:38,080 --> 00:25:41,800
God loves us because he made us.

296
00:25:41,800 --> 00:25:44,200
Not because he needs anything from us.

297
00:25:44,200 --> 00:25:55,220
I had to wrestle with this experience a few years ago when we were discussing the merge between Canvas and Grace.

298
00:25:55,700 --> 00:26:04,420
I got, but the board at Canvas gave me a three-month period sabbatical to just kind of get away and see if I even wanted to do whatever they were going to decide to do.

299
00:26:04,420 --> 00:26:15,500
And three months, it doesn't sound that long, but when you've spent your entire adult life constantly with to-do lists and schedules and busyness and opportunities and things that had to get done,

300
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:22,000
three months of nothing that had to get done was problematic for my soul, problematic for my identity.

301
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:27,860
Where do I get my identity from if I'm not doing something for someone that they need done?

302
00:26:27,860 --> 00:26:32,320
Efficiency, busyness, distraction.

303
00:26:32,320 --> 00:26:41,420
And a few months into that sabbatical, I was smacked in the face by this line of this modern worship song called Gyra.

304
00:26:41,420 --> 00:26:44,960
It says, I have never been more loved than I am right now.

305
00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:50,200
I wasn't holding you up, so there's nothing that I can do to let you down.

306
00:26:50,200 --> 00:26:53,660
This is what solitude reminds us of.

307
00:26:53,660 --> 00:26:58,880
When we withdraw away from the busyness and distraction and the noise and the efficiency of this world,

308
00:26:58,880 --> 00:27:02,820
and we just sit in God's presence, we are reminded that we are enough.

309
00:27:02,820 --> 00:27:08,740
That we are loved, not because of what we do, but because of who he is.

310
00:27:08,740 --> 00:27:15,160
All right, if you've made it this far without totally writing me off,

311
00:27:15,500 --> 00:27:18,560
or starting to scroll on your social media feeds,

312
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:23,520
or putting in your last-minute Super Bowl bets on DraftKings Sportsbook.

313
00:27:23,520 --> 00:27:26,960
Hashtag DKPartner.

314
00:27:26,960 --> 00:27:29,000
I'm kidding.

315
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:33,280
I have to say, I'm not getting paid by DraftKings to say that,

316
00:27:33,280 --> 00:27:35,660
or you guys will write some emails.

317
00:27:35,660 --> 00:27:41,140
All right, let's begin to land the plane by getting specific about what solitude is

318
00:27:41,140 --> 00:27:43,920
and how we can begin to practice it in our life.

319
00:27:43,920 --> 00:27:45,460
I would define solitude this way.

320
00:27:45,460 --> 00:27:51,840
Solitude is intentionally making room for God free from all their inputs or agendas.

321
00:27:51,840 --> 00:27:57,960
It's an intentional action to get away from inputs and agendas and to position ourself before God.

322
00:27:58,020 --> 00:28:01,000
It is us cultivating in our own lives an Eremos.

323
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:05,660
A desolate place, a faraway place.

324
00:28:05,660 --> 00:28:09,240
And most of us might not be able to make a desert happen,

325
00:28:09,240 --> 00:28:14,360
but we can cultivate a space that's free from agendas and inputs if we try hard enough.

326
00:28:14,360 --> 00:28:15,800
Let's break that definition down.

327
00:28:15,800 --> 00:28:16,400
Inputs.

328
00:28:16,400 --> 00:28:17,300
What do I mean by inputs?

329
00:28:17,300 --> 00:28:21,240
Well, most, again, talking about writing me off,

330
00:28:21,360 --> 00:28:24,820
most of you in here who are extroverts, you probably tuned me out a while ago.

331
00:28:24,820 --> 00:28:29,780
No, solitude isn't an extra credit activity for spiritual people,

332
00:28:29,780 --> 00:28:33,020
but it definitely is a spiritual practice just for introverts.

333
00:28:33,020 --> 00:28:35,180
That's probably what you've been saying, right?

334
00:28:35,180 --> 00:28:36,480
It's not for me.

335
00:28:36,480 --> 00:28:37,420
I'm an extrovert.

336
00:28:37,420 --> 00:28:38,820
That's the introverts thing.

337
00:28:38,820 --> 00:28:41,200
You guys probably tuned me out a while ago.

338
00:28:41,200 --> 00:28:45,820
There's no way I'm spending extended time alone by myself.

339
00:28:45,820 --> 00:28:51,100
But my introverts in the room, you likely have been eagerly following along,

340
00:28:51,360 --> 00:28:55,980
sweet, pastor said Jesus commands me to get away from people.

341
00:28:55,980 --> 00:28:56,500
I'm in.

342
00:28:56,500 --> 00:29:02,780
I can't wait to send the text back when the group invites me out for the, you know, whatever.

343
00:29:02,780 --> 00:29:04,960
Nope, pastor said I have to be alone.

344
00:29:04,960 --> 00:29:12,780
However, most introverts that I know love to be alone,

345
00:29:12,780 --> 00:29:16,940
but what they mean when they mean alone, I would not call solitude.

346
00:29:16,940 --> 00:29:20,880
Because most introverts like to be alone with inputs.

347
00:29:22,200 --> 00:29:25,720
They like to spend time with a great book or a great album or podcast,

348
00:29:25,720 --> 00:29:29,200
maybe some knitting needles or woodworking shop.

349
00:29:29,200 --> 00:29:36,560
But solitude is difficult because it's about eliminating all distraction from our life

350
00:29:36,560 --> 00:29:39,080
and making space to hear from God.

351
00:29:39,700 --> 00:29:41,960
This will make you extroverts feel a little bit better.

352
00:29:41,960 --> 00:29:45,480
Social scientists who studied this, they actually found that when they made a difference,

353
00:29:45,480 --> 00:29:48,080
when they distinguished solitude from aloneness,

354
00:29:48,080 --> 00:29:51,900
when they distinguished solitude as a place where there are no inputs or agendas,

355
00:29:51,900 --> 00:29:58,220
there was actually no greater preference for or enjoyment of solitude between introverts and extroverts.

356
00:29:58,780 --> 00:30:00,220
Everyone hated solitude.

357
00:30:00,220 --> 00:30:04,740
Again, equal opportunity offender.

358
00:30:04,740 --> 00:30:09,320
The reality is almost all Westerners hate the idea of solitude.

359
00:30:09,320 --> 00:30:13,360
We are afraid to be alone with our own thoughts and with God.

360
00:30:14,200 --> 00:30:20,740
We'd much prefer to distract or numb ourselves than be confronted with the realities of our finite lives

361
00:30:20,740 --> 00:30:22,320
in the presence of an infinite God.

362
00:30:22,320 --> 00:30:30,360
Solitude is an intentional space that we create for God, free from all inputs.

363
00:30:30,900 --> 00:30:34,880
It's a space where we can learn to be naked and unashamed before God.

364
00:30:34,880 --> 00:30:38,860
It's Genesis 3.

365
00:30:38,860 --> 00:30:44,140
Adam and Eve realize that they are finite, that they are sinners,

366
00:30:44,140 --> 00:30:48,980
that they have rebelled against God, and he is infinite and holy and just and righteous.

367
00:30:48,980 --> 00:30:53,980
And instead of leaning into his presence and acknowledging who they are and what they've done,

368
00:30:53,980 --> 00:30:55,100
they run away and hide.

369
00:30:55,100 --> 00:31:00,020
Solitude is the invitation to come back to God as we are.

370
00:31:00,900 --> 00:31:02,260
Naked and unashamed.

371
00:31:02,260 --> 00:31:08,600
In the presence of God, because of his incredible love for us.

372
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:12,040
Cal Newport says this, he says,

373
00:31:12,040 --> 00:31:19,200
Solitude requires you to move past reacting to information created by other people.

374
00:31:19,200 --> 00:31:24,480
And instead, on your own thoughts and experiences, wherever you happen to be.

375
00:31:24,480 --> 00:31:30,540
I don't know about you, but this is something that is extremely difficult for me

376
00:31:30,540 --> 00:31:31,280
to wrap my head around.

377
00:31:31,280 --> 00:31:34,720
We're constantly reacting to outside information.

378
00:31:34,720 --> 00:31:40,380
We're constantly, anything in our mind and our heart is us processing information that's happening to us

379
00:31:40,380 --> 00:31:44,780
or problems that we're trying to conquer, and we're always working in angle.

380
00:31:44,780 --> 00:31:51,200
Solitude is about us being ourselves before God, wherever we happen to be.

381
00:31:51,200 --> 00:31:56,100
He's not asking us to come cleaned up and ready for solitude.

382
00:31:56,560 --> 00:31:58,800
He's just asking us to show up.

383
00:31:58,800 --> 00:32:01,580
All right, inputs.

384
00:32:01,580 --> 00:32:02,440
Next, agendas.

385
00:32:02,440 --> 00:32:08,020
I grew up in a church culture that regularly preached the need for daily quiet time with God.

386
00:32:08,020 --> 00:32:10,060
Anybody else familiar with that terminology?

387
00:32:10,060 --> 00:32:11,920
A couple of us?

388
00:32:11,920 --> 00:32:12,180
Okay.

389
00:32:12,900 --> 00:32:14,080
Almost every Sunday.

390
00:32:14,080 --> 00:32:17,340
There wasn't a Sunday that didn't go by where we talked about quiet time with God.

391
00:32:17,340 --> 00:32:20,820
Now, cynics in the room might be thinking, wait a second, Ryan.

392
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:23,800
I thought you said our culture has totally forsaken solitude.

393
00:32:23,800 --> 00:32:28,220
You grew up in a church that regularly preached for you to have quiet time with God,

394
00:32:28,220 --> 00:32:30,580
and now you're telling me that they never taught you to do this?

395
00:32:32,500 --> 00:32:37,680
Although we used the language of quiet time, it was far from the practice of silence and solitude.

396
00:32:37,680 --> 00:32:42,240
It was a good thing, a beneficial thing, a thing that I should have done and should have been taught to do.

397
00:32:42,240 --> 00:32:47,880
But it was me coming to God with a devotional book, a verse-by-verse Bible study,

398
00:32:47,880 --> 00:32:51,960
a theological question that I was wrestling with and needed an answer to,

399
00:32:51,960 --> 00:32:55,760
or an intercessory prayer that I needed God to show up and answer.

400
00:32:56,600 --> 00:32:57,600
That is agendas.

401
00:32:57,600 --> 00:32:59,860
That is us coming to God with our agendas.

402
00:32:59,860 --> 00:33:01,080
I want to learn this thing.

403
00:33:01,080 --> 00:33:02,740
I want to be fed by this devotional book.

404
00:33:02,740 --> 00:33:03,440
I want this.

405
00:33:03,440 --> 00:33:04,100
I want that.

406
00:33:04,100 --> 00:33:06,900
It is us bringing our agendas to God.

407
00:33:06,900 --> 00:33:08,960
And all of these things are wonderful and right.

408
00:33:08,960 --> 00:33:13,660
But they are a time where we come to God with our agenda.

409
00:33:13,660 --> 00:33:17,520
We have something we want to learn, a question we hope to get clarity on,

410
00:33:17,520 --> 00:33:19,080
or a prayer request we want answered.

411
00:33:19,080 --> 00:33:23,320
And solitude is an invitation to something totally different.

412
00:33:24,160 --> 00:33:27,620
It is an opportunity for us to enter God's presence without an agenda.

413
00:33:27,620 --> 00:33:32,960
We allow Him to have time and space for His agenda in our lives.

414
00:33:32,960 --> 00:33:37,700
We create space for Him to speak to us by His Spirit.

415
00:33:37,700 --> 00:33:44,700
And I'm concerned that much of American Christian discipleship is us coming to God with our curriculum,

416
00:33:44,700 --> 00:33:46,740
with our agenda, and our desired outcomes.

417
00:33:46,740 --> 00:33:50,340
And we don't give God much space at all to speak for Himself in our lives.

418
00:33:51,820 --> 00:34:00,360
How healthy would my relationship with my wife or my closest friends be if we were never alone together?

419
00:34:00,360 --> 00:34:02,320
Always in a crowded room.

420
00:34:02,320 --> 00:34:08,900
Or if we did get alone together and I came with a scripted list of questions to ask.

421
00:34:08,900 --> 00:34:11,200
Or a book to discuss.

422
00:34:11,200 --> 00:34:13,640
None of those things are bad.

423
00:34:13,700 --> 00:34:16,740
But if that was the only thing we ever did alone together was,

424
00:34:16,740 --> 00:34:19,360
Hey, I have a list of six questions I need to ask you.

425
00:34:19,360 --> 00:34:22,600
And let's read three chapters of this book and talk about it next week.

426
00:34:22,600 --> 00:34:24,600
Sounds intimate, right?

427
00:34:24,600 --> 00:34:35,320
Solitude is an intentional space we cultivate to be with God and to listen to His voice with no other agendas.

428
00:34:37,180 --> 00:34:37,600
All right.

429
00:34:37,600 --> 00:34:39,440
Should we get practical?

430
00:34:39,440 --> 00:34:40,880
Okay.

431
00:34:40,880 --> 00:34:42,000
That's good.

432
00:34:42,000 --> 00:34:46,100
Probably a lot to digest if you've never considered this practice before.

433
00:34:46,100 --> 00:34:49,720
You might be thinking, this sounds nice, but I live in Winchester and not Galilee.

434
00:34:49,720 --> 00:34:51,360
Where am I going to find a desert?

435
00:34:51,360 --> 00:34:54,080
And when do I have time to go there?

436
00:34:54,080 --> 00:34:55,900
Right?

437
00:34:55,900 --> 00:34:57,120
It's a good question.

438
00:34:57,120 --> 00:34:57,940
It's a great question.

439
00:34:59,060 --> 00:35:05,020
And the reality is this concept of Ramos, it doesn't have to be outdoors and in the desert.

440
00:35:05,020 --> 00:35:10,400
It can be just a lonely place where you remove yourself from all other inputs or agendas.

441
00:35:10,400 --> 00:35:12,100
And this will be difficult.

442
00:35:12,100 --> 00:35:14,900
Especially if you've never practiced it before.

443
00:35:14,900 --> 00:35:16,860
I heard a pastor talking about this.

444
00:35:16,860 --> 00:35:21,640
And when he preached it, he went to the creek and he got a mason jar full of creek water.

445
00:35:21,640 --> 00:35:25,940
And he shook it up at the beginning of his sermon and set it on the table.

446
00:35:25,940 --> 00:35:28,020
The water was murky.

447
00:35:28,020 --> 00:35:28,760
Right?

448
00:35:29,400 --> 00:35:31,880
There's all kinds of residue and things going on.

449
00:35:31,880 --> 00:35:37,520
And it took until the very end of his sermon for all of the residue to settle and for the water to be clear.

450
00:35:37,520 --> 00:35:42,780
This is often how solitude works in our lives, especially if we're not used to practicing it.

451
00:35:42,780 --> 00:35:45,280
We go away to a lonely place free.

452
00:35:45,280 --> 00:35:48,940
And we try and free ourselves from inputs or agendas with God.

453
00:35:48,940 --> 00:35:54,540
And you're constantly going to be bombarded with all of the thoughts and distractions and busyness of your life.

454
00:35:54,540 --> 00:35:55,720
Did I put this in the calendar?

455
00:35:55,720 --> 00:35:57,320
Did I ask this person about that thing?

456
00:35:57,320 --> 00:35:58,020
And that's okay.

457
00:35:58,580 --> 00:35:59,340
It's okay.

458
00:35:59,340 --> 00:36:06,440
But we have to train ourselves to remove those thoughts, to let them be before God.

459
00:36:07,500 --> 00:36:21,340
Although I think extended periods of silence and solitude outdoors is a great goal for us to pursue, every one of us can create space and time in and around our house to get alone with God, free of all inputs and agendas, even if it's just for a few minutes.

460
00:36:22,220 --> 00:36:30,960
My current routine involves a comfortable section of my couch where I have to face away from the TV because if not, I'll be thinking about all the things I could be watching on TV.

461
00:36:31,740 --> 00:36:32,820
Real people, real people, real people, real lives.

462
00:36:32,820 --> 00:36:40,280
With my stage of life, I have to either get up early before Rory gets up or I have to stay up late after he goes to bed.

463
00:36:40,280 --> 00:36:47,320
And in order for me to get up early, which is a struggle, you can ask my wife about how many times I don't, I silence the alarm.

464
00:36:47,320 --> 00:36:51,460
Silence is loose language because I'm like fumbling for it.

465
00:36:51,600 --> 00:36:54,320
And then I get it quiet and I usually set two alarms.

466
00:36:54,320 --> 00:36:58,580
So by the time I quiet the first alarm, there's like two minutes in between before the next one goes off.

467
00:36:58,580 --> 00:37:07,680
But in order for me to get up early, I found that I have to say no to staying up late scrolling or binge watching things or I'm never going to get up early.

468
00:37:07,680 --> 00:37:10,300
These are intentional decisions that I have to make.

469
00:37:10,300 --> 00:37:11,480
Sometimes I do it well.

470
00:37:11,480 --> 00:37:12,920
Sometimes I don't do it at all.

471
00:37:12,920 --> 00:37:15,660
I am not up here bragging about how great I am at solitude.

472
00:37:15,660 --> 00:37:19,040
I'm inviting you to come along on the journey with me.

473
00:37:20,800 --> 00:37:23,580
You might have to limit your time of scrolling.

474
00:37:23,580 --> 00:37:30,180
You might have to say no to getting sucked into a new show that will immediately for me become a binge problem.

475
00:37:30,180 --> 00:37:34,000
Anybody else can't start a show without watching the entire show on a weekend?

476
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:36,940
Okay, just me, great.

477
00:37:36,940 --> 00:37:39,660
You guys are really encouraging this morning.

478
00:37:39,660 --> 00:37:47,700
And I found like I used to always have a show that I was like wanting to get through.

479
00:37:48,320 --> 00:37:50,960
And I thought like, I can't not have a show.

480
00:37:50,960 --> 00:37:53,080
And when you get to a point where you don't have a show, what do you do?

481
00:37:53,080 --> 00:37:54,900
You go find one.

482
00:37:54,900 --> 00:37:55,720
You talk to your friends.

483
00:37:55,720 --> 00:37:57,180
What's the show that I should be watching?

484
00:37:57,180 --> 00:37:59,820
And I thought I'd be missing out on something.

485
00:37:59,820 --> 00:38:04,840
But I'm to the point where I'm no longer looking for shows to get sucked into because I know what it'll do.

486
00:38:04,840 --> 00:38:06,600
And I know the value it'll add to my life.

487
00:38:06,600 --> 00:38:07,760
And it's not much.

488
00:38:08,240 --> 00:38:11,560
And it's keeping me from things that matter far more.

489
00:38:11,560 --> 00:38:16,640
I don't know exactly what it'll look like for you.

490
00:38:16,640 --> 00:38:21,040
Maybe you start by just turning off the radio or taking out your headphones on your commute.

491
00:38:21,040 --> 00:38:25,180
It can look a million different ways tailored to your personality and stage of life.

492
00:38:25,680 --> 00:38:30,480
But the one constant for all of us is that it will require intentional planning and sacrifice.

493
00:38:30,480 --> 00:38:37,460
You will likely have to say no to something else that isn't necessarily bad in order to say yes to something better.

494
00:38:38,900 --> 00:38:42,800
Like much of life with Christ, at first it will feel like dying to yourself.

495
00:38:42,800 --> 00:38:50,860
But at some point the scales will tip and you'll realize that death to self actually leads you to the abundant life that you were created for.

496
00:38:50,860 --> 00:38:55,060
I love how Ruth Haley Barton puts it.

497
00:38:55,060 --> 00:38:57,200
Mercy, you and Liz can come up if you're ready.

498
00:38:57,200 --> 00:38:59,140
Forgot to give her a cue.

499
00:38:59,140 --> 00:39:00,720
I would have given her this quote as a cue.

500
00:39:00,720 --> 00:39:03,020
So now you guys are hearing that process happening out loud.

501
00:39:03,020 --> 00:39:05,820
Ruth Haley Barton says this.

502
00:39:05,820 --> 00:39:09,380
The invitation to solitude and silence is just that.

503
00:39:09,380 --> 00:39:18,240
It is an invitation to enter more deeply into the intimacy of the relationship with the one who waits just outside the noise and busyness of our lives.

504
00:39:18,240 --> 00:39:26,580
It's an invitation to communication and communion with the one who is always present even when our awareness has been dulled by distraction.

505
00:39:26,580 --> 00:39:32,280
It's an invitation to the adventure of spiritual transformation in the deepest places of our being.

506
00:39:32,280 --> 00:39:39,700
And an adventure that will result in great freedom and authenticity and to surrender to God that we have yet to experience.

507
00:39:39,700 --> 00:39:47,260
Like I said in my intro, I think most of us in the room, we long for deeper intimacy with God.

508
00:39:47,260 --> 00:39:48,860
We long for spiritual transformation.

509
00:39:48,860 --> 00:39:53,740
We long to hear from God and be used by him in ways that we could only imagine.

510
00:39:54,800 --> 00:40:06,960
What if this practice of solitude from the life of Jesus is a resource on that journey that has long been ignored by our fast-paced, efficiency-driven, overstimulated, and overly distracted culture?

511
00:40:06,960 --> 00:40:12,840
What might you and I be missing out on by ignoring this example and invitation from Jesus?

512
00:40:13,800 --> 00:40:20,280
And what might we stand to gain by swimming against the currents of our culture and sacrificially saying yes to this invitation?

513
00:40:20,280 --> 00:40:25,420
Now, as we wrap up, I do want to make two things really clear.

514
00:40:25,420 --> 00:40:32,720
First, we mentioned it in Haley's quote,

515
00:40:33,040 --> 00:40:57,240
If you show up to solitude, show up to solitude because you want to say yes to the invitation of Jesus to get alone, away from the busyness and distraction of this world, away from your agenda of what you want God to do in your life,

516
00:40:57,240 --> 00:41:02,800
and you just want to give him space to speak, if that's what you want to do, then solitude is an invitation for you to come there.

517
00:41:02,800 --> 00:41:05,020
But don't come there for any other reason.

518
00:41:05,020 --> 00:41:08,920
Don't do it because you think it'll make you a better person or a more sanctified Christian.

519
00:41:08,920 --> 00:41:13,000
Do it because you're receiving an invitation from the one who created you and loves you.

520
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:17,780
Secondly, solitude is not a silver bullet for spiritual growth.

521
00:41:17,780 --> 00:41:22,460
We talked about infomercials a couple weeks ago.

522
00:41:22,460 --> 00:41:24,540
I don't want to oversell these spiritual practices.

523
00:41:24,540 --> 00:41:29,520
These are uphill climbs, all of them from fasting to prayer to generosity and now to solitude.

524
00:41:29,520 --> 00:41:37,660
They are all long, lifelong journeys and invitations that will slowly but surely over time conform us into a person of Christ-like love.

525
00:41:37,660 --> 00:41:44,260
You're not going to show up to solitude this week and then come back and be like, I just heard from Jesus so much this week.

526
00:41:44,260 --> 00:41:47,300
He gave me a full paragraph.

527
00:41:47,300 --> 00:41:48,140
I wrote it down.

528
00:41:48,140 --> 00:41:51,480
This is a quote from God because I spent 10 minutes in solitude, right?

529
00:41:51,480 --> 00:41:52,820
It's not a silver bullet.

530
00:41:53,620 --> 00:41:56,280
Spiritual growth, it doesn't work like that.

531
00:41:56,280 --> 00:41:58,960
And spiritual growth is not our work, it's God's.

532
00:41:58,960 --> 00:42:05,220
Solitude is simply a space we carve out to encounter God, not just with our minds but with our whole selves.

533
00:42:05,220 --> 00:42:09,640
It's an intentional space we make for him to do his work in us.

534
00:42:12,640 --> 00:42:19,560
I ask Marissa and Liz to lead us in a song of prayer that I think is at the heart of the invitation to solitude.

535
00:42:19,560 --> 00:42:24,880
It's a prayer for more of God's presence and more of his transforming work in our lives.

536
00:42:24,880 --> 00:42:27,900
So feel free to do a couple of things.

537
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:30,460
Feel free to sit and let this prayer be prayed over you.

538
00:42:30,460 --> 00:42:33,600
Feel free to stand, to join in and sing.

539
00:42:33,600 --> 00:42:34,580
It's a repetitive song.

540
00:42:34,660 --> 00:42:36,180
If you don't know it, it won't be hard to learn.

541
00:42:36,180 --> 00:42:50,460
Or maybe you take this time to think about your schedule this week, to think about your habits, your patterns, your lifestyle, and think about what it would take, what decisions you would need to make in order to say yes to this invitation.

542
00:42:53,360 --> 00:43:01,620
Just like any time you get an invitation to do anything you find value of, you often have to look at your schedule and say, this is something I want to prioritize.

543
00:43:01,620 --> 00:43:06,040
And in order to prioritize this, I'm going to have to say no to this or this or this.

544
00:43:06,040 --> 00:43:08,720
This is an invitation from Jesus.

545
00:43:08,720 --> 00:43:09,700
So take this time.

546
00:43:09,700 --> 00:43:22,480
Use it to be prayed over, to pray, or to think about what things you might need to do to intentionally make room for God, free from all other inputs or agendas.

547
00:43:22,480 --> 00:43:24,440
As I'll be back up to dismiss us in prayer.

548
00:43:24,440 --> 00:43:28,280
I just.
